<article class="prose-view">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-body-secondary">Last updated: {{updated}}</p>
{{#unless ads}}
    <p><strong>In short:</strong> Wayhop has no accounts, uses no cookies and doesn't track you. It only counts how many pages are opened in each browser language, nothing about who opened them.</p>
{{/unless}}
{{#if ads}}
    <p><strong>In short:</strong> Wayhop has no accounts and doesn't track you itself; it only counts how many pages are opened in each browser language. It shows ads from Google, which uses cookies as described under <a href="#advertising">Advertising</a>.</p>
{{/if}}

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Your language</strong> (English or Greek), saved in your browser so pages open in it next time.</li>
        <li><strong>The last three metro stations you picked,</strong> so the subway planner can offer them first next time.</li>
        <li><strong>The metro stations, bus stops and bus lines you starred as favourites,</strong> so the subway planner and the bus page offer them first.</li>
        <li><strong>Where you left each tool</strong> (the stations or the bus stop you were looking at), while the tab or app stays open, so the navigation takes you back there.</li>
        <li><strong>Whether you chose the light or the dark colours,</strong> if you did, so the pages open in them.</li>
        <li><strong>Whether you closed the explanation of what the train shows,</strong> if you did, so it stays closed on the metro pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Whether you closed the offer to install the app</strong> with "Not now", if you did, so it isn't shown again.</li>
        <li><strong>Copies of pages and timetables,</strong> saved by the app so it works offline.</li>
        <li><strong>Your location,</strong> if you allow it: your browser uses it to find the bus stops and metro station nearest to you. The last one is kept on your device for 10 minutes, so the next page can use it straight away, even without a signal. It isn't sent to Wayhop's server. Wayhop also notes that you allowed it, so the next page doesn't have to ask first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You can clear all of this in your browser's settings for this site.</p>

    <h2>What reaches our server</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The bus stops you're looking at.</strong> To show live arrivals, the bus page asks the server for nearby stops, and the stops you starred, by their number. Nearby stops show roughly where you are, but they aren't kept with anything that identifies you. Arrivals are kept per stop for a few minutes and shared by everyone.</li>
        <li><strong>The addresses and places you search for.</strong> To find them, the planner sends what you type to our server as you type, and the server looks it up itself. It isn't kept, it isn't in the web server's logs, and it goes to nobody else.</li>
        <li><strong>Your IP address, for one minute.</strong> To share OASA's live data fairly, and to keep address search working for everyone, the server counts each address's requests over one minute, in memory only.</li>
        <li><strong>The language your browser prefers, as a count.</strong> When a page is opened, the server adds one to that day's count for the first language your browser asks for (English, German, French…). Only the daily totals are kept, to see which languages to add next. Nothing about who opened the page is kept.</li>
        <li><strong>Standard web server logs.</strong> Like any website, our host's web server logs each request (IP address, page, time and browser) for security and to fix problems.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Wayhop's server, not your device, asks OASA for live data, and those requests carry only the stop number.</p>

{{#if ads}}
    <h2 id="advertising">Advertising</h2>
    <p>To cover its costs, Wayhop shows a few ads from Google AdSense. They're always labelled "Advertisement", come after the page's content, and are never placed in live arrivals or route results.</p>
    <p>Google and its partners use cookies and similar technologies to show ads, measure them and prevent fraud, and, if you agree, to personalise them. In the European Union, Google asks for your choice before using cookies for ads. If you don't agree, you may still see ads that aren't personalised.</p>
    <p><button type="button" id="ad-choices" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary rounded-pill" data-ad-client="{{adClient}}" hidden>Change your ad choices</button></p>
    <ul>
        <li>{{#external href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"}}How Google uses information from sites that use its services{{/external}}</li>
        <li>{{#external href="https://myadcenter.google.com/"}}Google's ad settings{{/external}}, to turn off personalised ads</li>
    </ul>

{{/if}}
    <h2>Other websites</h2>
    <p>Walking directions open Google Maps, and announcements link to the OASA and STASY websites. Their own privacy policies apply there.</p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>Under the GDPR you can ask what we hold about you and ask us to delete it. Wayhop keeps almost nothing that identifies you, but <a href="/contact">contact us</a> with any question. You can also complain to the {{#external href="https://www.dpa.gr/"}}Hellenic Data Protection Authority{{/external}}.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
{{#unless ads}}
    <p>If anything here changes, for example if Wayhop starts showing ads, this page will say so first.</p>
{{/unless}}
{{#if ads}}
    <p>If anything here changes, this page will say so first.</p>
{{/if}}
</article>
